100
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,000
Scout MasterCG in seven minutes or less?

101
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,000
When Ben Powell started the scouting movement, one of the things he had his eye on

102
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:58,000
was helping young people learn the benefits and responsibility of being good citizens.

103
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:02,000
And I want to talk a little bit about the relationship between being a good citizen

104
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:04,000
and being a good leader,

105
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Because I think the two are kind of synonymous. a good leader and a good citizen

106
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:11,000
both have a sense of individual responsibility

107
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,000
and the desire to be of service to others.

108
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
I think when we engage youth in leadership,

109
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:21,000
we are engaging them in learning the skills of citizenship.

110
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:25,000
Now, naturally, we aren't all destined to become leaders in the classic sense of the term

111
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,000
by directing others or leading a group.

112
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:31,000
There's much more to leadership than simply being in command,

113
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:35,000
And I think leadership has four basic components.

114
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:39,000
The first component of leadership is tied very closely to the Scout motto:

115
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:44,000
be prepared, because that component of leadership is self-leadership.

116
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:48,000
Before you lead anybody else, you have to be able to care for your own

117
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:50,000
spiritual, mental and physical needs.

118
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:54,000
If Scouts don't care for themselves, if we don't care for ourselves,

119
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:58,000
we end up limiting our preparedness and our ability to help others.

120
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:01,000
So the first person we lead is ourselves.

121
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:05,000
And then the next component that builds on this is leading by following,

122
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,000
by doing your best

123
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:10,000
And following cooperatively requires a positive attitude

124
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:14,000
and positive actions that actually contribute to what's going on.

125
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:17,000
The next component is assisting leadership:

126
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:20,000
Not just looking after ourselves, not just following,

127
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:24,000
but assisting our fellow Scouts and Scouters by being kind and friendly

128
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,000
and helpful at every opportunity,

129
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:30,000
Looking for opportunities to serve other people.

130
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:34,000
And then, finally, the fourth component is directive leadership.

131
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:37,000
Scouts who lead themselves and follow cooperatively

132
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:42,000
and help their fellow Scouts are prepared to become directive leaders.

133
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:45,000
And directive leaders- that's a classic kind of leader,

134
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,000
The guy with the patch on his shoulder, whether it's a patrol leader

135
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:51,000
or a senior patrol leader. these are the people that you look to

136
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:55,000
for direction and leadership and definition as to what's happening.

137
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:59,000
It's not a choice of whether you want to be a leader or not.

138
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,000
All of the Scouts are leaders in one way or another.

139
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:05,000
You can't be a Scout without being a leader.

140
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:08,000
In our younger Scouts we want them to have the ability

141
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:12,000
to look beyond themselves and to see how their actions

142
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:14,000
and attitudes affect others.

143
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:17,000
We help them associate good actions and attitudes

144
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,000
with the Scout Oath and Law

145
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:22,000
And we help them develop that first component,

146
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:24,000
that self-leadership.

147
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,000
We want them to have a growing sense of basic altruism

148
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:30,000
and the desire to serve others and to lead

149
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
by being a cooperative follower.

150
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:35,000
We see the evidence of that when they start to find satisfaction

151
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,000
in helping others more than just fulfilling

152
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,000
their own needs and wants and desires.

153
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
In our younger Scouts they have great capacity

154
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:46,000
for assisting leadership, to help with simple tasks

155
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:48,000
and to work cooperatively together.

156
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:52,000
And that all builds onto that directive leadership

157
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
where they can take on direct roles.

158
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
Now they might not take on a direct role

159
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,000
as the guy that wears the patch.

160
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:02,000
They can lead in games and ceremonies and activities

161
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:05,000
with the help of older Scouts and appropriate oversight.

162
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:08,000
And in our older Scouts we want that self-leadership idea

163
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,000
to be very strong.

164
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,000
We want them to have a strong sense of how their actions

165
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
and attitudes affect others

166
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:18,000
And to be readily able to associate the Scout Oath

167
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,000
and Law with their actions and attitudes.

168
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,000
Cooperation and readiness to help others

169
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
should come with little or no prompting.

170
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:28,000
They should have a heightened sense of fairness and empathy

171
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:30,000
and be growing more and more tolerant

172
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
and accepting of different points of view

173
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
and levels of ability.

174
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
In our older Scouts the ideals of service

175
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
and cooperation are strong.

176
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:42,000
Assisting with leadership comes very naturally to them.

177
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
They should have some pride and group accomplishments

178
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:46,000
over individual achievements

179
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
and they associate their well-being

180
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
with the well-being of everybody around them.

181
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
And those first three components-

182
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:56,000
that ability to lead yourself,

183
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,000
the ability to be cooperative

184
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
and the ability to lend a hand-

185
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:03,000
all form the fourth component,

186
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:06,000
which is that directive leadership.

187
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,000
I think we're successful in building good citizens

188
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:10,000
and good leaders,

189
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,000
not so much necessarily gauging by metrics

190
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
the things that result from their leadership.

191
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
That can certainly be an important indicator.

192
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
What we're looking for are Scouts

193
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:23,000
that have the moral and ethical obligations

194
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
of the Scout, Oath and Law-

195
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:28,000
as a central guiding point in their lives.

196
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
They're welcoming, supportive and helpful

197
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,000
towards their fellow Scouts

198
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
and confident in their outlook and relationships.

199
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,000
They have fully formed ideals of service

200
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:41,000
and they readily subordinate their needs

201
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
to the needs of others.

202
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
They have a desire to offer help

203
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
and to make a difference

204
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
And they have a keen sense of responsibility

205
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
to lead fairly and well,

206
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
with little or no oversight.

207
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
And we want to order things

208
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
in our mentoring and advising

209
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
so that they feel as though

210
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
they can seek advice and assistance

211
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
and we can help them get on the right track.

212
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
Nobody can be a Scout without being a leader,

213
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
even at the very basic level

214
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
of just leading yourself,

215
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
And I think we need to recognize that

216
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
and recognize that development in our Scouts

217
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:16,000
and help them develop those four components of leadership:

218
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,000
The self-leadership, cooperative leadership,

219
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
assisting leadership

220
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:24,000
and then assuming a position of responsibility

221
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
where they have some directive leadership involved.

222
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
In doing that,

223
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:31,000
we are making a fantastic contribution

224
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
to every community that they're involved in.

225
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
We're building good citizens

226
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
and we're achieving

227
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
the aims of Scouting.

